From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: Meta: Cargo Cult censure?

I know I'm not one of the "major posters", being relatively new to the
list, but here's my take on the current "cargo cult/flying yogi"
situation.

It seems to me that the PPL we use on the list contains an inherent
bias in favor of irrational postings. Namely, there is no additional
cost associated with proposing something absurd, while there may be
cost associated with defending against the absurd.

A completely ad hoc example:

	Poster A: "Since Extropians believe in boundless expansion,
		   how come they don't advocate a genetic program
		   to produce infinitely long penises in men?"

	Poster B: "Because that's stupid."

Poster A might be viewed as foolish, but Poster B would probably
receive more censure.

Note that I would not be comfortable kicking seemingly irrational
posters off the list per se. After all, sometimes a seemingly
irrational question turns out to be insightful and useful. (Out of the
mouths of babes, and all that...) Nor would I be in favor of any other
"punitive" measures against them, for the same reason. But I did want
to point out that the costs involved in these kinds of discussions seem
asymmetrical to me.

