Date: Fri, 12 Mar 1993 12:08:49 -0500 (EST)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: CHALLENGE: What I do *not* believe

After reading Mr. May's last post, I see that it would facilitate matters
to get rid of some of the straw men a discussion of scientism vs. anything
labelled `mysticism' raises.  Therefore, I submit for Mr. May's consideration
a sort of non-credo --- a list of things I do *not* believe in.

I assert that this non-credo is representative of the belief systems of most
self-described "techno-pagans".  This latter claim is, of course, open to
factual refutation by Mr. May.

I do not believe in pyramid power, crystal energy, Atlantis, astrology,
flying yogis, or psychic TV jamming.
   (These are the sorts of things I've called "vulgar superstitions" in the
past, and I will continue to do so.)

I do not believe in the existence of disembodied spirits or what William
James called an "objective correlative" of godhood.
    (I routinely invoke gods and goddesses; however, I regard them as
essentially *intersubjective* phenomena, albeit one with powerful
objective consequences in the behavior of humans.)

I reject "faith" and "revelation".  That is, I do not believe there is
any reliable way to attain knowledge of the objective universe other
than by experiment and reason.
    (The qualifier "objective" is important.)

I do not believe that the self survives death, either in an afterlife
or by reincarnation.
   (However, I admit that neither of these possibilities is outright excluded
by evidence, and have been attracted by speculation that some *memories* ---
though not identity --- may survive death to be carried in other people.)

I do not regard the existence of telepathy, telekinesis, ESP or any other
"psychic power" as established fact.
    (Though I concede that some of these things may be real; in particular,
I have had some experiences that were damn difficult to explain other
than by either an utterly implausible degree of coincidence or telepathy.)

I do not believe in any "supernatural" phenomena; in fact, I consider the
term and concept of "supernatural" to be nonsense on the order of "square
circle".
    (That is, not only do I reject all "supernatural" explanations, I
consider that the word depends on an unanalyzed self-contradiction in
the world-view of anyone using it seriously.)
