Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 12:54:20 EST
From: lubkin@apollo.hp.com
Subject: SOC: Taboos (Was: Cannibalism)

["We", in this posting, will alternately mean "Extropians", "people in our
culture", or "human beings", depending on context.]

I think we should, as a general principle, examine closely ideas
for which we or others have immediate revulsion and try to understand 
why we or others feel that way.  Where appropriate, we might change 
our views or try to change other peoples'.  In all cases, I think we 
are richer for exploring the subject.  For example, cannibalism, torture,
neurosuspension, incest, bestiality, homosexuality, sex with children, 
eugenics, snot, assassination, deliberate body mutilation (clitorectomy,
circumcision, pierced ears, noses, cheeks, lips) and PeeWee Herman.

[I add as a proviso to posterity and eavesdroppers that I do not necessarily
advocate any of the above, and may occasionally take positions I do not
believe in in order to liven the discussion.]

I'll start with cannibalism, since that's the topic of the day, and then I'll
touch lightly on some of the others.  

Some posters explicitly stated that they found the subject of cannibalism
disgusting.  Others implicitly acknowledged either that they or the world at 
large find the subject disgusting by making jokes about it.

Why do we have a taboo against cannibalism in this culture?  My guess is that
the meme reduces the spread of disease, and discourages people from preying 
on their neighbours.  But these are operational details, and not inherent to
eating human.

Is there anything inherently wrong with it?  What is wrong with growing
human tissue for consumption in vitro?  What is wrong with 'recycling' healthy
accident victims or executed prisoners?

Perry says humans aren't kosher:

    No, as human beings are not split-hooved four legged ungulates that
    regurgitate and chew our cud.
    
What happens if human genes are introduced into bovines?  Is it kosher?  Is
there anything wrong with it?  At what point does a genetic mixture become 
offensive?  

Other topics:

I am not sure what the origin of the taboo against sex with children is.
Is it universal?  I've read science fiction stories set in futures where 
parents are expected to introduce their children to sex, but I don't know
enough anthropology to know if there are actual cultures where this is so.
Why do we find this abhorrent?

I've read articles and sf suggesting that assassination is vastly
preferable to full-scale war.  Understandably, those in power have an interest
in propagating an anti-assassination meme.  But why is that meme successful?

Revulsion for bestiality is presumably tied to long-standing human desires to
believe we are superior to lower animals in all respects, and that we are
somehow not animals as well.  Is there anything wrong with it?

This also harkens back to the discussion of whether Perry? could torture
animals in the privacy of his own home under a PPL system.  Personally, I
am dismayed by the way that our legal system divides the world into human and
non-human, with rights and no rights, and nothing in between.  Why does a
human imbecile or newborn baby have more rights than Koko, the gorilla who 
signs and who has scored 70 on human (and human-biased) IQ tests?  I'd like to
see a scale of rights that are species-independent, based on factors like 
intelligence, sapience, sentience, and capacity to feel pain.  And be able to 
stop Perry from torturing puddy-tats.

(This is clearly another thread, and any responses should so indicate.)

Anyway, are there any non-human species that can be considered to be capable
of giving informed consent to sex with a human?



